---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Database & Migrations">
  <h1>Database &amp; Migrations</h1>
  <p>
    Identity's primary backend is <strong>Postgres</strong>
    (<code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres</code>, the default). The in-memory
    driver keeps no durable state and can skip this page. On the Postgres
    driver Identity stores
    its durable state in PostgreSQL, and the schema is managed by versioned,
    embedded migrations applied with
    <a href="https://github.com/golang-migrate/migrate">golang-migrate</a>. The
    <code>.sql</code> files are compiled into the binary, so the image is
    self-contained — operators never ship migration files separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Connection</h2>
  <p>Three environment variables configure the datastore:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN</code> — required. Standard libpq URL.</li>
    <li><code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_MAX_CONNS</code> — pool size. Default <code>25</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE</code> — apply pending migrations on
      boot. Default <code>false</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code code={`GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN="postgres://identity:secret@db:5432/identity?sslmode=require"`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>First-time setup</h2>
  <p>Three steps. The database starts empty; migrations create every table.</p>

  <h3>1 — Create an empty database</h3>
  <Code code={`createdb identity
# or: CREATE DATABASE identity; with a dedicated, least-privileged role
# that owns the schema (the migration runner needs DDL on this database).`} lang="bash" />

  <h3>2 — Point identity at it</h3>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN="postgres://identity:secret@db:5432/identity?sslmode=require"`} lang="bash" />

  <h3>3 — Apply the schema</h3>
  <p>
    Either let the service migrate on first boot, or run migrations as a
    separate step (see <a href="#running-migrations">Running migrations</a>).
    The simplest first run:
  </p>
  <Code code={`docker run --rm \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN="postgres://identity:secret@db:5432/identity?sslmode=require" \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE=true \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:latest`} lang="bash" title="first boot — creates the full schema" />
  <p>
    On this boot the runner applies every migration from <code>0001</code> to
    the latest in order, then the service starts. It is idempotent — booting
    again is a no-op.
  </p>
  <p>
    After migrations are in place, the service seeds a control-plane
    <strong>default project</strong> on every boot (idempotently): id
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID</code> (default <code>default</code>),
    mapped onto the <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code> storage scope. If
    you run migrations as a separate step rather than on boot, run them
    <em>before</em> starting the server — otherwise boot fails fast with a hint
    to run <code>identity migrate</code>, because the <code>projects</code>
    table (migration <code>0013</code>) does not yet exist.
  </p>

  <h2>How migrations work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Versioned + forward-ordered.</strong> Files are numbered
      (<code>0001_init.up.sql</code>, <code>0002_…</code>, …) with a matching
      <code>.down.sql</code>. They apply strictly in order.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tracked.</strong> A <code>schema_migrations</code> table records
      the current version, so only <em>pending</em> migrations run on any boot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Idempotent.</strong> Re-running against an up-to-date database
      does nothing (<code>ErrNoChange</code> is swallowed).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-replica safe.</strong> golang-migrate takes a Postgres
      advisory lock for the duration of a run, so if ten replicas boot at once
      exactly one applies the migrations and the rest wait, then no-op. No
      double-apply, no race.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="running-migrations">Running migrations</h2>
  <p>Two supported modes — pick one per environment.</p>

  <h3>A — Auto on boot (single-container / dev)</h3>
  <p>
    Set <code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE=true</code>. The service migrates
    before serving traffic. Simplest, and safe even with several replicas
    (the advisory lock serialises them). Recommended for single-container
    deployments and local/dev.
  </p>

  <h3>B — Explicit step (production / fleets)</h3>
  <p>
    Keep <code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE=false</code> and run migrations
    <em>once, before</em> rolling out the new application version, so the schema
    is ready before any new-version replica serves. In Kubernetes this is a
    <code>Job</code> (or an init container) gated ahead of the rollout:
  </p>
  <Code code={`apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: identity-migrate
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0   # the version you are deploying
          args: ["migrate"]                          # run migrations, then exit
          env:
            - name: GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN
              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: identity-db, key: dsn } }`} lang="yaml" title="migrate-before-deploy Job" />
  <blockquote>
    The <code>migrate</code> subcommand (<code>identity migrate</code>) runs the
    embedded migrations and exits — it is the supported way to drive Mode B from
    the image alone, as the Job above does. A typo'd subcommand exits non-zero
    rather than starting the server, so a one-shot migrate Job never hangs.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Upgrading to a new release</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A release that changes the schema ships <strong>new</strong> numbered
      migration files. Older ones never change.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each release's notes carry a <strong>Migrations</strong> line: "none", or
      the versions added and whether any are long-running / destructive.
    </li>
    <li>
      On upgrade, only the <em>pending</em> migrations apply (mode A on boot, or
      mode B as your step); already-applied versions are skipped.
    </li>
    <li>
      Migrations are <strong>forward-only</strong> in production. <code>.down</code>
      files exist for local rollback and tests; prefer a forward fix in prod.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Data migrations — your existing data is preserved</h2>
  <p>
    Migrations run arbitrary SQL, so a change that must <em>transform</em>
    existing rows ships the data step in the same migration as the schema step.
    Existing data is updated in place, never dropped. The convention for a
    change that touches live data is <strong>expand → backfill → contract</strong>:
  </p>
  <Code code={`-- 0013_add_email_verified.up.sql  (expand)
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email_verified boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false;

-- backfill existing rows in the same migration
UPDATE users SET email_verified = true
WHERE id IN (SELECT user_id FROM email_verification_tokens WHERE consumed_at <> 0);`} lang="sql" title="schema + data in one migration" />
  <p>For a <strong>zero-downtime</strong> change to a column live code reads, split it across releases so the old and new app versions both work during the rollout:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Expand</strong> (release N): add the new column/table, nullable; start writing it.</li>
    <li><strong>Backfill</strong> (release N, batched): populate existing rows.</li>
    <li><strong>Contract</strong> (release N+1, after every replica is on N): switch reads, then drop the old column.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Before a destructive or long-running production migration: <strong>take a
    backup / snapshot</strong> (e.g. <code>pg_dump</code> or a volume snapshot),
    and run large backfills in batches to avoid long table locks.
  </p>

  <h2>The Projects / Tenants migration (redesign)</h2>
  <p>
    When the Project / Tenant / Domain model lands it arrives as ordinary
    migrations, and existing single-tenant data is preserved, not reset:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a <strong>default Project</strong> row is created, mapped to your current <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code> storage scope;</li>
    <li><code>project_id</code> is <strong>backfilled</strong> on every existing row to that default Project;</li>
    <li><strong>Tenants</strong> are formed (latent) from the email domains already present in <code>users</code>, skipping public providers;</li>
    <li>the retired <code>organizations</code> / <code>organization_memberships</code> tables are exported for the workspace service, then dropped.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is a standard expand-backfill-contract sequence — your users, tokens,
    passkeys, and audit history carry forward unchanged.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
